text:
The course philosophy
AI is a powerful collaborator, not a substitute for analytical judgment. A prompt that says “analyze my sales data” leaves too many decisions unstated: Which period? Which measure of sales? Which level of aggregation? What counts as a customer? What should the final output look like?
The better workflow is:

understand the structure of the data and the decision to be made;
describe the task precisely to an AI coding tool;
run the proposed code in a controlled environment;
inspect the output, warnings, and errors;
revise the code and test the revision; and
communicate assumptions and limitations alongside the result.

Foundational R knowledge makes every step more reliable. You do not need to memorize every function, but you do need to recognize what objects, filters, joins, summaries, and plots are doing.

Data contract
Ask the AI assistant to create a reproducible hypothetical data frame called sales with:

region: character label;
month: integer from 1 through 12;
year: integer;
revenue: numeric dollars before returns; and
returned_revenue: numeric dollars returned.

Each row represents one region-month. Net revenue means revenue minus returned revenue.

Testing protocol
Use a tiny hand-check. If a region has 2025 net revenue of 100 and 2026 net revenue of 120, its growth should be 0.20, or 20 percent. If a region has no 2025 revenue, it should not receive an ordinary percentage-growth ranking without an explicit policy.
Check the generated output with:

the number of regions;
the number of region-year combinations;
the smallest and largest growth values;
any missing growth values; and
the raw totals for one selected region.

Deliverable
Prepare a short finance memo with:

the top-ranked region and its growth rate;
the definition of net revenue;
the comparison period;
any region excluded or flagged because of missing or zero prior-year revenue; and
one limitation of ranking regions by percentage growth alone.

Debugging joins and summaries
Joins and summaries deserve extra attention because they can produce plausible but inflated results.
For a join, check:

the intended key;
whether the key is unique in each table;
the join type;
the number of rows before and after; and
whether unmatched records matter.

For a summary, check:

the observation unit;
the grouping columns;
whether the measure should be summed or averaged;
whether the denominator is weighted; and
whether missing values are excluded intentionally.

When the code runs but the answer is wrong
Use a result audit:

print the first and last rows;
count records before and after each filter;
inspect a few records manually;
compare a total with an independent calculation;
check the smallest and largest values;
test a known case; and
ask a stakeholder whether the metric matches their definition.

The best debugging question is often not “How do I fix this error?” It is “What would have to be true for this result to be correct?”

The project review protocol
For every project:

read the business request and identify the decision-maker;
copy the exact prompt and inspect the AI’s plan;
paste the generated code into an R script, Quarto document, or the Sandbox;
test row counts, keys, filters, calculations, and output order;
ask the AI to explain any error before applying a fix; and
write a stakeholder note that states the result, definition, and limitation.

The objective is not to obtain the longest script. It is to produce an analysis whose inputs, transformations, and claims can be inspected.

Testing protocol
Create a hand-check customer with three completed orders of 200, 150, and 200 dollars. The customer should have total spend of 550 and belong to the Core segment. Add a cancelled order and verify that it does not change the segment if the business definition says completed orders only.
Also inspect:

total rows before and after the date filter;
total customers before and after missing-ID handling;
the sum of segment customer counts;
the sum of segment spend; and
the minimum and maximum customer spend.

Deliverable
Prepare a marketing brief with:

the count and share of customers in each segment;
the total spend represented by each segment;
the definition of value used;
one action the marketing team could consider for each segment; and
one reason the segmentation should not be treated as a permanent customer identity.

Data contract
Ask the AI assistant to create a reproducible hypothetical data frame called transactions with:

store_id: character store identifier;
transaction_id: character transaction identifier;
transaction_date: Date;
sales: numeric dollars;
units: integer units sold; and
checkout_minutes: numeric time from checkout start to completion.

Each row represents one transaction.
The analysis should:

exclude rows missing store_id or transaction_id and report how many were excluded;
check for duplicate transaction IDs;
flag negative sales;
calculate unique transaction count, total sales, total units, average basket value, average units per transaction, and median checkout time by store;
flag stores with median checkout time above 8 minutes; and
distinguish descriptive evidence from causal claims.

Deliverable
Prepare an operations dashboard note with:

the store-level summary;
the stores flagged for operational review;
the quality issues found;
two descriptive observations; and
one statement explaining why the analysis does not establish causation.

A complete pipeline with mtcars
The built-in mtcars dataset contains one row per car model. For this teaching example, treat the cars as a product portfolio. We will:

keep cars with at least six cylinders;
keep the columns needed for the analysis;
calculate horsepower per unit of weight;
group cars by cylinder count;
calculate group-level metrics; and
sort the groups by average fuel efficiency.

#| '!! shinylive warning !!': |
#|   shinylive does not work in self-contained HTML documents.
#|   Please set `embed-resources: false` in your metadata.
#| standalone: true
#| components: [editor, viewer]

library(shiny)
library(dplyr)

car_summary &lt;- mtcars |&gt;
  tibble::as_tibble(rownames = "model") |&gt;
  filter(cyl &gt;= 6) |&gt;
  select(model, mpg, cyl, hp, wt) |&gt;
  mutate(power_to_weight = hp / wt) |&gt;
  group_by(cyl) |&gt;
  summarise(
    cars = n(),
    average_mpg = mean(mpg),
    average_hp = mean(hp),
    average_power_to_weight = mean(power_to_weight),
    .groups = "drop"
  ) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(average_mpg))

ui &lt;- fluidPage(
  h3("Product portfolio summary"),
  p("The table compares car groups with six or more cylinders."),
  tableOutput("summary_table")
)

server &lt;- function(input, output, session) {
  output$summary_table &lt;- renderTable({
    car_summary
  }, digits = 1)
}

shinyApp(ui, server)

7. Conditional logic
Use if and else for a decision involving one value. Use ifelse() or dplyr’s if_else() for vectorized decisions.

profit &lt;- 73000

if (profit &gt; 0) {
  "Profitable"
} else {
  "Loss"
}

performance &lt;- ifelse(sales &gt;= 40000, "Above threshold", "Below threshold")
performance

Write the threshold and its meaning down. A label such as “high value” is not self-explanatory unless the business rule is documented.

Foundation checklist
Before moving to data wrangling, you should be able to:

assign and inspect named objects;
distinguish numeric, character, logical, integer, and date values;
create and inspect vectors;
identify and handle missing values;
subset vectors and data frames;
read a function call and its arguments;
explain a conditional rule; and
identify which packages a script requires.

When an AI assistant proposes code, these foundations let you ask better questions about what the code receives, what it returns, and what the business result means.
